From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 05:46:23 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] kosmickrisp: probe a quiet Metal queue before calling it
 stopped

A backgrounded iOS app is denied the GPU, and the Metal command queue of
a denied app rejects every submission. Each rejected commit is reported
to its feedback handler within milliseconds, as a timeout for a
submission the GPU admitted and ran none of, and the driver survives
that: it completes the signals the failure owed and retires the queue at
the next submit. A timeline signal enqueued without a command buffer
behind it has no feedback, and the queue drops it without a word.

On an A15, a Kernarium guest drawing with glmark2 was held off the GPU
5 s after the app entered the background, which is two seconds past the
point where iOS denies it. The console shows 38 rejected commits and 24
retirements, the last one a second before the hold, and then nothing
from the driver for 30 s. Then it reports that the queue
accepted a timeline signal for value 2, delivered nothing, reported no
failure and holds no unanswered work, and loses the device; the guest's
GL client, waiting in vkQueueWaitIdle, gets VK_ERROR_DEVICE_LOST and
never draws again. The same run held at 3 s recovers. Nothing could
tell the two apart: the retirement clears the failure count, the queue
held nothing it had not answered for, and a failure count of zero with
nothing unanswered is what a queue that has stopped looks like.

A timeline waiter whose signal has been overdue for
KK_QUEUE_PROBE_DELAY_NS, one second, on a queue that reports no failure
and holds nothing it has not answered for, now has the queue committed
an empty command buffer (kk_queue_probe), at most one a second. The
probe is an ordinary submission with a serial and a report. A queue that
rejects it reports a failure, the failure completes the signals the
queue owes as any other does, and the wait ends about a second after it
began. A queue that runs it reports nothing and loses nothing, and a
queue held up behind work only the host can release answers it when the
host does. The probe never completes a signal itself, so a queue that
has really stopped still loses the device at the bound it had.

The probe commits from the waiter's thread. The submit thread therefore
holds a mutex for the whole of kk_queue_submit and the probe takes it
with a try: a queue that is being submitted to is not quiet, and its
replacement cannot move under the probe.

The test queue_denied reproduces the denial on a Mac by replacing the
Metal queue's commit with a rejection reported the way iOS reports it
and its signalEvent:value: with a drop, for as long as a flag says the
GPU is denied. In after-failure a rejected commit is recovered and a
signal-only submit follows on the queue that replaced the failed one; in
first-op the signal-only submit is the first thing the denied queue
sees. Both must end their wait in about a second, keep the device, and
serve work again when the GPU is back. In legit-wait nothing is denied
and a commit and its signal wait behind a wait only the host can
release: the queue must not be probed, nothing may be completed early,
and the device must stay.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
